Title:
My_Custom_EDR (Custom EDR Engine with Debug API + Inline Hooking)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- A C++ “mini-EDR” project that monitors a target process using the Windows Debugging API to catch DLL/module injection at runtime (e.g., via `LOAD_DLL_DEBUG_EVENT`).  
- It focuses on early module-load inspection: validating module paths, hashing/signature checks, and parsing PE headers to decide whether a loaded DLL is suspicious.  
- When a threat is detected, it performs “active remediation” by patching the injected module in memory (e.g., overwriting an entry point with a `ret`) to make the DLL fail quietly while keeping the main process running.  
- The repo also demonstrates inline API hooking to intercept and optionally manipulate/log parameters for selected APIs, including `LdrLoadDll` and `MessageBoxA`.  
- It discusses practical Windows loader constraints (notably loader lock) and shows an agent pattern that runs synchronously in `DllMain` with `DisableThreadLibraryCalls`.  
- Useful for red teamers/malware researchers to understand EDR-style telemetry and for blue team/EDR engineers as a learning reference for user-mode monitoring and response mechanics.

Technical Focus:
- Windows Debugging API event loop (`LOAD_DLL_DEBUG_EVENT`) for module-load monitoring  
- Inline API hooking (user-mode) of `LdrLoadDll` / `MessageBoxA`  
- In-memory patching / entry-point modification for blocking (“ret” patch)  
- PE header parsing and module validation (path checks, spoofing detection)  
- Hashing/signature matching using Windows CNG (`bcrypt.lib`)  
- Loader lock / `DllMain` execution constraints

Use Cases:
- Build a lab-grade EDR-like monitor to study DLL injection and module load behavior
- Prototype user-mode detections around runtime module loads and suspicious DLL paths
- Experiment with inline hooks for telemetry collection and parameter manipulation
- Test “soft-blocking” techniques by patching injected modules without crashing the host process
- Educational reference for Windows loader internals and debugging-based monitoring

Keywords:
Windows Debugging API, LOAD_DLL_DEBUG_EVENT, EDR, C++, user-mode monitoring, DLL injection, module load, LdrLoadDll, inline hooking, API hooking, DllMain, loader lock, DisableThreadLibraryCalls, PE parsing, entry point patching, in-memory patch, ret instruction, bcrypt.lib, CNG, hashing, std::unordered_set